Presence of this file is how kernel/Makefile discovers a package:

    KERN_PKG_DIRS = $(filter-out ../pkgs/core/panels/src/,
                       $(sort $(dir $(wildcard ../pkgs/core/*/src/KERN))))

So creating pkgs/core/codec/src/KERN is the whole of the kernel integration --
every .c in this directory is picked up and built with the kernel's freestanding
flags (-nostdinc -nostdlib -nodefaultlibs -mno-sse -mno-sse2). No Makefile edit
is needed, and none should be made: a package that has to be registered by hand
is a package that silently stops building.

The decoders here are written to that constraint deliberately:

  * no libc headers, so nothing may use <string.h>, <stdlib.h> or <math.h>.
    Allocation and string functions come through codec_alloc.h and codec_str.h,
    which map onto kernel/mm.h and kernel/string.h.
  * no float, because -mno-sse -mno-sse2 leaves no float ABI. codec_wav.c
    converts IEEE 754 binary32 with integer shifts for this reason.
  * no libc malloc in the hot path; CODEC_MAX_DIM / CODEC_MAX_PIXELS bound every
    allocation up front, since a corrupt width/height header is the standard
    way a decoder becomes an out-of-bounds write.

See pkgs/core/codec/src/codec.h for why the decoders are hand-written rather
than the lodepng/tjpgd copies already in the tree under kernel/lvgl/, and
pkgs/core/codec/tests/run.sh for the host-side verification.